    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">
      How to Verify a Chinese Wire Mesh Factory Before Sending Money: 7 Checks
    </h1>

    <p class="text-theme-secondary text-lg mb-8">
      Last updated: September 2026 | 10 min read
    </p>

    <!-- Answer First -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
      <p class="text-theme-primary text-lg leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Quick answer:</strong> Before sending any deposit to a Chinese wire mesh supplier, run these seven checks: (1) verify the business license on China's National Enterprise Credit Information system and confirm manufacturing scope; (2) demand a live video factory tour, not an edited promo; (3) order samples and test them—mesh count, wire diameter, weight against the <a href="/tools/woven-wire-mesh-weight-calculator/" class="text-theme-primary underline">weight calculator</a>; (4) match the bank account name to the registered company name; (5) ask for redacted bills of lading proving export history; (6) book a third-party inspection ($150–400) for orders above $20,000; (7) learn the factory-vs-trader signals so you know who you are actually dealing with. Together these take about two days and eliminate most fraud, most trading-company markups, and most quality surprises. Start with a pre-screened list from our <a href="/factories/" class="text-theme-primary underline">verified factory directory</a> to skip the riskiest cases entirely.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- TOC -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Table of Contents</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-theme-secondary">
        <li><a href="#check1" class="hover:text-theme-primary">Check 1: Business License and Registration</a></li>
        <li><a href="#check2" class="hover:text-theme-primary">Check 2: The Live Video Factory Tour</a></li>
        <li><a href="#check3" class="hover:text-theme-primary">Check 3: Sample Testing Against the Weight Calculator</a></li>
        <li><a href="#check4" class="hover:text-theme-primary">Check 4: Bank Account Name Match</a></li>
        <li><a href="#check5" class="hover:text-theme-primary">Check 5: Export Records and Bills of Lading</a></li>
        <li><a href="#check6" class="hover:text-theme-primary">Check 6: Third-Party Inspection</a></li>
        <li><a href="#check7" class="hover:text-theme-primary">Check 7: Factory vs Trading Company Signals</a></li>
        <li><a href="#faq" class="hover:text-theme-primary">FAQ</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Intro -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Anping County and the wider <a href="/wire-mesh-hebei/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">Hebei wire mesh region</a> contain over 500 real manufacturers—and an unknown number of trading companies, resellers, and outright fraudsters who look identical online. A polished Alibaba storefront, a catalog of certificates, and fast English replies prove nothing about what happens after your deposit lands. Verification is not paranoia; it is the step that separates the 95% of transactions that go fine from the ones that end in lawyers.
      </p>
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        These seven checks are ordered by cost and effort: the first five are free and take minutes to hours. By the time you reach check six, you should already be fairly confident. For the complete buying workflow around these checks, see our <a href="/sourcing-guide/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">sourcing guide</a> and the step-by-step <a href="/blog/how-to-import-wire-mesh-from-china/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">import guide</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Check 1 -->
    <section id="check1" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Check 1: Business License and Registration</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Every legitimate Chinese company holds a business license (营业执照) with a 18-digit Unified Social Credit Code. Ask the supplier for a photo or scan of theirs. Then verify it yourself—do not accept their word or a screenshot of a verification page:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li>Go to the National Enterprise Credit Information Publicity System (gsxt.gov.cn—the official registry; free, Chinese-language) or a third-party aggregator like Qcc.com or Tianyancha which translate the key fields.</li>
        <li>Enter the company name exactly. Confirm the company exists, is "in operation" (存续), and note the <strong>registered capital</strong>, <strong>registration date</strong>, and <strong>legal representative</strong>.</li>
        <li>Check the <strong>business scope</strong> (经营范围). You want characters like 生产 (produce) or 制造 (manufacture). A scope limited to 销售 (sales) or 进出口 (import/export) means trading company—fine, but they should not be posing as a factory.</li>
        <li>Compare the registered address with the address on their proforma invoice and website. Mismatches need an explanation.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Registration date matters more than people think. A company registered 14 months ago claiming "15 years of experience" is either a re-brand (ask why) or a lie. Neither is an automatic disqualifier—many good Anping factories are young—but the claim and the record must reconcile.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Red Flag</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          Refuses to send the license, sends one with a name that differs from the bank account, or the credit code returns "no record found." Any one of these ends the conversation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Check 2 -->
    <section id="check2" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Check 2: The Live Video Factory Tour</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Anyone can download a factory video. The test is whether the supplier can show you <em>their</em> production floor <em>live</em>, on your terms. Book a WeChat or WhatsApp video call and ask for specific things:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li>Walk from the gate to the office—read a sign or the company name plaque aloud and ask them to hold the phone on it.</li>
        <li>Show the wire-drawing or incoming wire station, the welding lines, the galvanizing line (if they claim in-house galvanizing), and the finished-goods warehouse.</li>
        <li>Ask them to stop at a running machine producing your product type—50×50 mm welded mesh, for example—and count wires with you on camera.</li>
        <li>Ask an unscripted technical question mid-tour: "What wire tolerance do you hold on this line?" A real production person answers in seconds.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        What you are looking for: multiple machines of the same type (a one-machine "factory" is a workshop at best), raw wire stock, and semi-finished goods. What gives away a trader: a tour of one rented corner, "the main workshop is being repainted today," or a video call that suspiciously freezes whenever you ask to see a specific machine.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        If you cannot do a live tour, ask for a dated video showing today's newspaper plus a request you made up—e.g., "hold the camera on the mesh count gauge for ten seconds." Traders outsource this awkwardly; factories do it in one take.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Check 3 -->
    <section id="check3" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Check 3: Sample Testing Against the Weight Calculator</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Samples are cheap—$20–100 plus courier—and they answer the question every other check dances around: is the product actually what was quoted? When the sample arrives, test it in this order:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Test</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Tool Needed</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Pass Criterion (typical)</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">What Cheating Looks Like</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Mesh count / aperture</td>
              <td class="p-3">Mesh counter gauge or ruler</td>
              <td class="p-3">±3% of nominal</td>
              <td class="p-3">One extra wire per inch (5% heavier-looking mesh, 10% less open area)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Wire diameter</td>
              <td class="p-3">Micrometer (0.01 mm)</td>
              <td class="p-3">±5% of nominal</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.02–0.05 mm undersize—hard to see, big material saving</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Total weight per m²</td>
              <td class="p-3">Scale + <a href="/tools/welded-wire-mesh-weight-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">weight calculator</a></td>
              <td class="p-3">Within ~3% of theoretical</td>
              <td class="p-3">Consistent shortfall = thin wire, light zinc, or both</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Material grade (stainless)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Magnet screen + XRF at local lab (~$50)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Matches grade on quote (304 / 316)</td>
              <td class="p-3">201-grade substituted for 304—rusts within months</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Zinc coating weight</td>
              <td class="p-3">Coating gauge, or strip test</td>
              <td class="p-3">Per contract (e.g., ≥275 g/m² HDG)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Electro-galvanized (25 g/m²) shipped as "hot-dip"</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        The weight check is the most powerful and the most skipped: a mesh roll cannot lie to a scale. Compute theoretical weight for the quoted spec, weigh the sample, and divide. If a "4.0 mm wire, 50×50 mm mesh" panel weighs 8% less than theory, the wire is thinner than quoted—full stop. Repeat this exact check at pre-shipment inspection on a random roll from the container.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Approve the sample in writing and reference it in the PO: "Mass production must match approved sample #X." That sentence is your quality contract.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Check 4 -->
    <section id="check4" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Check 4: Bank Account Name Match</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        This is the single highest-stakes check because it is the one fraudsters cannot fake without tripping a wire. On the proforma invoice, look at the beneficiary name of the T/T bank account:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Pass:</strong> beneficiary equals the registered company name (in Chinese pinyin or English) from the business license you verified in Check 1.</li>
        <li><strong>Fail:</strong> beneficiary is an individual's name, a Hong Kong shell with an unrelated name, or a "sister company" with no documented relationship.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Two real-world threats this catches. First, the fake-supplier scam: a storefront that is a complete fiction, collecting 30–50% deposits into a personal account before disappearing. Second, the email-hijack scam: a legitimate factory's mailbox is compromised, and the attacker sends "updated bank details" mid-negotiation from a lookalike address. Defense for both: any change of bank details is confirmed by phone with a contact whose number you obtained before the change appeared—and ideally by video call.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Never</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          Pay Western Union, "friends and family" PayPal, or crypto to a wire mesh supplier. Irreversible payment rails plus a beneficiary mismatch is the exact signature of every deposit-disappearance story we have encountered. Standard terms are 30% T/T deposit, 70% after passing pre-shipment inspection.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Check 5 -->
    <section id="check5" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Check 5: Export Records and Bills of Lading</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        A real exporter generates paperwork. Ask for:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Recent bills of lading</strong> (2–3, client data redacted) showing shipments to your region. The BL shows shipper, consignee country, port, and cargo description—enough to confirm volume and destinations without exposing clients.</li>
        <li><strong>Export registration</strong>—Chinese exporters hold customs registration; a factory exporting for years will state its customs code.</li>
        <li><strong>Destination-market paperwork</strong> relevant to you: CE/EN declarations for fence products into the EU, ASTM test reports into the US, or Form A / certificate of origin templates.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Cross-examine what you receive. A BL listing "mesh fence, 26 tons, Xingang to Felixstowe" last month is strong evidence of an operating export business. A supplier who can produce nothing but insists they "export worldwide" is telling you something. Also note the product names on the BLs: if they say only "wire mesh" and you are buying PVC-coated fence panels with folded edges, the factory may be learning your product on your deposit.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Check 6 -->
    <section id="check6" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Check 6: Third-Party Inspection</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        For orders above $20,000—or any first order with a new supplier—pay for an independent inspection. It is the only check where a professional whose fee does not depend on the shipment passing actually looks at your goods:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Factory audit</strong> (before ordering, $150–400): SGS, Bureau Veritas, TÜV, QIMA, or a local Anping agency verifies the license, facility, capacity, and quality system on site. This is the gold standard for the factory-vs-trader question.</li>
        <li><strong>During-production inspection</strong> (mid-production, $100–200): catches material and weave problems while they are still fixable.</li>
        <li><strong>Pre-shipment inspection</strong> (before balance payment, $150–300): random-roll checks of mesh count, wire diameter, coating weight, dimensions, packaging, and quantity against your PO and approved sample.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Give the inspector your full spec plus the weight calculation so they can re-run it on the factory floor. One practical note: a legitimate factory will schedule an inspection without drama. A supplier who stalls, claims "the factory is too busy this week," or wants to substitute their own photos has just answered every question in this article.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Check 7 -->
    <section id="check7" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Check 7: Factory vs Trading Company Signals</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Trading companies are not evil—good ones add value on logistics and communication—but you deserve to know which you are paying, because a trader adds 5–15% and removes your direct quality leverage. The signals:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Signal</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Real Factory</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Trading Company</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Business scope</td>
              <td class="p-3">生产/制造 (manufacturing)</td>
              <td class="p-3">销售/进出口 (sales/export-import)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Product catalog</td>
              <td class="p-3">Narrow: 3–8 related mesh products</td>
              <td class="p-3">Broad: mesh + fences + nails + tools + pipe</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Technical answers</td>
              <td class="p-3">Instant, in numbers ("we hold ±3% on aperture")</td>
              <td class="p-3">"Let me check with the engineer"—every time</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Video tour</td>
              <td class="p-3">Walks the floor, names machines</td>
              <td class="p-3">Office call, or "factory visit needs 3 days notice"</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Address</td>
              <td class="p-3">Industrial zone address in Anping/Raoyang/Hengshui area</td>
              <td class="p-3">Office tower address (Shijiazhuang, or Anping town center)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Price on identical spec</td>
              <td class="p-3">Quoted tightly; negotiable on volume</td>
              <td class="p-3">Often lower at first (to win), then "cost adjustments"</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        If you want to skip the detective work, our <a href="/factories/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">verified factory directory</a> lists pre-screened Anping manufacturers, and the <a href="/sourcing-guide/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">sourcing guide</a> walks through shortlisting, RFQ, and negotiation in sequence. The <a href="/tools/inquiry-template-generator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">inquiry template generator</a> makes sure every supplier answers the same questions, which by itself surfaces most impostors.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How much does it cost to verify a Chinese wire mesh factory?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Checks 1, 2, 4, 5, and 7 are free—license lookup, video tour, bank match, export records, and signal reading cost nothing but time. Samples run $50–180 with courier. A third-party factory audit costs $150–400, and pre-shipment inspections $150–300. Budget $300–600 total for thorough verification on a $20,000+ order—roughly 2% of order value to protect the other 98%.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Is a Gold Supplier badge or Trade Assurance enough verification?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            No. Platform badges confirm a company paid for membership and passed a basic documentation check—not that they own a factory or will ship quality goods. Treat badges as the starting line, then run the seven checks. Trade Assurance does add payment protection, but claims take weeks and require documentation; it is a safety net, not a substitute for verification.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Can a factory refuse third-party inspection?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            They can, and some legitimate small workshops find inspections disruptive. But for a first order over $20,000, "no third-party inspection" is a disqualifier—full stop. On repeat orders with proven suppliers, you can relax to the factory's own photos/videos with a tape measure in frame, but the first transaction deserves eyes on the goods before the balance payment.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What is the fastest verification I can do if I'm in a hurry?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Fifteen-minute version: license lookup on gsxt.gov.cn (Check 1), a spontaneous live video call where you ask to see one machine and one warehouse corner (Check 2), and the bank beneficiary match on the proforma invoice (Check 4). If all three pass, order samples immediately and run Check 3 while production slots open. This catches the overwhelming majority of outright fraud.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Should I just use a sourcing agent instead of verifying myself?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            An agent makes sense if you are buying from multiple factories or need in-China QC, but verify the agent too—the same seven checks apply, and ask which factories they represent (many agents are paid by factories, not you). Self-verification is very achievable for single-product buyers; the checks above were designed to be run from a laptop anywhere. Our <a href="/factories/" class="text-theme-primary underline">factory directory</a> and <a href="/sourcing-guide/" class="text-theme-primary underline">sourcing guide</a> exist precisely so you do not have to pay 3–10% commission for what a careful afternoon accomplishes.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

